  <symbol id=\"i-tool\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M14.7 6.3a4 4 0 0 0-5.4 5.4L3 18v3h3l6.3-6.3a4 4 0 0 0 5.4-5.4l-2.5 2.5-2.5-2.5z\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-clip\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><rect x=\"5\" y=\"4\" width=\"14\" height=\"17\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M9 4V3h6v1M9 10h6M9 14h6M9 18h3\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-check\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"m5 12.5 4.5 4.5L19 7.5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-minus\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M6 12h12\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-info\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"9\"/><path d=\"M12 11v6M12 7.5v.5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-star\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M12 2.5l2.9 6.1 6.6.8-4.9 4.6 1.3 6.6L12 17.3l-5.9 3.3 1.3-6.6-4.9-4.6 6.6-.8z\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-down\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><path d=\"M12 4v12M6 10l6 6 6-6M5 20h14\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-cert\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><rect x=\"3\" y=\"4\" width=\"18\" height=\"12\" rx=\"2\"/><path d=\"M7 8h10M7 11h6M15 16v5l2-1.5 2 1.5v-5\"/></symbol>\n  <symbol id=\"i-family\" viewBox=\"0 0 24 24\"><circle cx=\"7\" cy=\"6\" r=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"17\" cy=\"6\" r=\"2.5\"/><circle cx=\"12\" cy=\"12\" r=\"2\"/><path d=\"M3 20c0-4 2-7 4-7s4 3 4 7M13 20c0-4 2-7 4-7s4 3 4 7\"/></symbol>\n</svg>\n\n", "header": "<header class=\"site-head\">\n    <a class=\"logo\" href=\"/en\" aria-label=\"Ocean Way, home\"><svg viewBox=\"2 2 116 116\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><defs><clipPath id=\"logo-clip\"><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"47.01\"/></clipPath></defs><g clip-path=\"url(#logo-clip)\" fill=\"currentColor\"><line x1=\"0\" y1=\"72.3\" x2=\"120\" y2=\"54.6\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"11\"/><path d=\"M28 59 C36 36 72 23.5 118 32 L118 51 C84 41 47 43.5 28 59Z\"/></g><circle cx=\"60\" cy=\"60\" r=\"52.5\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"11\"/></svg></a>\n    <nav class=\"site-nav\" aria-label=\"Main\">\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#courses\">Courses</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#passages\">Passages</a>\n      <a class=\"navl on\" href=\"/en/theory\">Theory</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"/en#fleet\">Fleet</a>\n      <a class=\"navl\" href=\"#\">Prices</a>\n    </nav>\n    <div class=\"site-tools\">\n      <details class=\"lang\" translate=\"no\"><summary class=\"round\" aria-label=\"Language: English\">EN</summary><ul class=\"lang-menu\"><li><a href=\"/en/theory/man-overboard\" hreflang=\"en\" lang=\"en\" aria-current=\"true\">English<span>EN</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/ru/theory/man-overboard\" hreflang=\"ru\" lang=\"ru\">Русский<span>RU</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/de/theory/man-overboard\" hreflang=\"de\" lang=\"de\">Deutsch<span>DE</span></a></li><li><a href=\"/es/theory/man-overboard\" hreflang=\"es\" lang=\"es\">Español<span>ES</span></a></li></ul></details>\n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      \n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" id=\"themeBtn\" aria-label=\"Theme\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-theme\"/></svg></button>\n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Saved\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-heart\"/></svg></button>\n      <button class=\"round\" type=\"button\" aria-label=\"Account\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-user\"/></svg></button>\n    </div>\n  </header>", "main": "\n    <article>\n      <header class=\"art-head\">\n        <nav class=\"crumbs\" aria-label=\"Breadcrumb\"><ol><li><a href=\"/en\">Home</a></li><li><a href=\"/en/theory\">Theory</a></li><li aria-current=\"page\">Radio and safety</li></ol></nav>\n        <h1 class=\"display\">Man overboard: getting someone back</h1>\n        <p class=\"lead\">Prevention comes first, but every crew member should know the drill: what to do in the first seconds, when to call for help, how to return under engine or sail, and how to lift the person aboard.</p>\n        <div class=\"byline\"><span class=\"meta\" style=\"color:var(--ink)\">Ocean Way instructors</span><span class=\"meta\">Updated 1 Nov 2026</span><span class=\"meta\">6 min read</span><span class=\"lvl\">All crew</span></div>\n      </header>\n      <div class=\"art-grid\">\n        <div class=\"art-body\">\n          <details class=\"art-mobile-toc band\"><summary>On this page<svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-chev\"/></svg></summary><ol class=\"toc-list\"><li><a href=\"#prevention\">Staying on board</a></li><li><a href=\"#first-seconds\">The first seconds</a></li><li><a href=\"#mayday\">When to call for help</a></li><li><a href=\"#engine\">Recovery under engine</a></li><li><a href=\"#quick-stop\">Quick-stop and heave-to</a></li><li><a href=\"#reach-tack-reach\">Reach, tack, reach</a></li><li><a href=\"#aboard\">Getting them aboard</a></li><li><a href=\"#cold\">Cold shock and hypothermia</a></li><li><a href=\"#practise\">Practise it on the water</a></li></ol></details>\n          <div class=\"band summary\"><span class=\"kicker\">In short</span><ul><li>Clip on, wear a lifejacket and keep one hand for the boat.</li><li>Shout, throw the lifebuoy and danbuoy, point and press MOB.</li><li>Under engine, check for lines in the water and keep the propeller away from the person.</li><li>Under sail, use the quick-stop or reach-tack-reach, with a final approach on a close reach.</li></ul></div>\n          <section id=\"prevention\">\n<h2>Staying on board</h2>\n<p>Getting someone back from the water is hard, even for a practised crew, so the best man overboard (also called person overboard) is the one that never happens.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>One hand for yourself, one for the boat.</b> When you move about the deck, keep hold of something solid: a grabrail, a shroud (a wire holding up the mast) or the boom when it is secured.</li>\n<li><b>Lifejackets.</b> Wear one when the skipper asks, and always at night, in rough weather and in the dinghy. It keeps your face clear of the water.</li>\n<li><b>Clip on.</b> At night and in rough weather, wear a harness and clip your tether to the jackstays (strong webbing lines that run along each side deck from bow to stern) before you leave the cockpit.</li>\n<li><b>Move carefully.</b> Stay low, use the windward (upwind) side deck, and never relieve yourself over the side. Use the heads (toilet) below.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"first-seconds\">\n<h2>The first seconds</h2>\n<p>When someone falls in, the boat sails away from them fast: at six knots, about three metres every second. The first moments decide how the recovery goes.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><b>Shout.</b> Call \"Man overboard\" loudly, and say which side, so the whole crew and the helm know at once.</li>\n<li><b>Throw.</b> Throw the horseshoe lifebuoy, the danbuoy (a floating pole with a flag and light that is easy to see in waves) and the lifesling if you carry one, as close to the person as you can. They give the person something to hold and mark the spot.</li>\n<li><b>Point.</b> One crew member points at the person with an outstretched arm and does nothing else. A head in the water vanishes behind the first wave if nobody keeps it in sight.</li>\n<li><b>Mark the position.</b> Press the MOB button on the chart plotter or GPS. It stores the position and shows the bearing and distance back to it.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Only then does the skipper choose how to get back, under engine or under sail.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"mayday\">\n<h2>When to call for help</h2>\n<p>A man overboard is grave and imminent danger to life. If the person is back aboard within a minute or two, you may not need outside help. But if you lose sight of them, cannot get back quickly, the water is cold or the crew is small, send a DSC distress alert and a Mayday at once. Rescuers can only help if they know.</p>\n<p>The exact words are covered in our article on VHF radio procedure. Give one crew member this job, so the helm and the spotter can keep doing theirs. If the person is recovered after all, cancel the alert.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"engine\">\n<h2>Recovery under engine</h2>\n<p>Motoring back is often the quickest and most controlled method. Before you start the engine, check that no lines are in the water: a sheet round the propeller leaves you with no engine at the worst moment.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Furl or drop the headsail, and sheet the mainsail in tight or drop it.</li>\n<li>Start the engine and motor back towards the person.</li>\n<li>Make the final approach slowly from downwind, heading into the wind, so the boat stops easily.</li>\n<li>Aim to bring the person alongside the windward bow, well forward of the stern.</li>\n<li>Put the engine in neutral before you reach them, so the propeller has stopped turning, and keep it there while they are close to the boat.</li>\n</ol>\n<aside class=\"note warn\"><b>Watch out</b><p>A turning propeller can cause terrible injuries. Never bring the stern towards a person in the water, and keep the engine in neutral whenever they are near it. If the person has to come aboard over the stern, stop the engine.</p></aside>\n</section>\n<section id=\"quick-stop\">\n<h2>Under sail: quick-stop and heave-to</h2>\n<p>The quick-stop aims to stop the boat right away, as close to the person as possible.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>As soon as the alarm is raised, tack immediately without releasing the headsail sheet. This is sometimes called a crash tack.</li>\n<li>The headsail is now backed (held on the wrong side), and the boat heaves to: she lies almost stopped, drifting slowly sideways.</li>\n<li>From there the person is close by. The skipper can drift down towards them, sail a short loop back, or furl the headsail and recover under engine after checking for lines.</li>\n</ol>\n</section>\n<section id=\"reach-tack-reach\">\n<h2>Under sail: reach, tack, reach</h2>\n<p>The reach-tack-reach method, sometimes called the figure of eight, brings the boat back under full control with sails that you can use as a brake.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Put the boat on a beam reach (the wind across the side) and sail away for several boat lengths, enough room to manoeuvre.</li>\n<li>Tack, and sail back on a broad reach, aiming at a point a few boat lengths downwind of the person.</li>\n<li>From there, turn up onto a close reach and head straight for them for the final approach.</li>\n<li>On this course you can ease the sheets until the sails flap, which slows the boat. Pull them in a little to keep moving.</li>\n<li>Stop with the person on the leeward side, just forward of the shrouds, where the deck is lowest.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The test of a good approach is simple: if you can let the sails flap completely and still point at the person, you are on the right course. If the sails will not flap, you are not far enough downwind of the person. Bear away and go round again rather than arrive too fast.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"aboard\">\n<h2>Getting them back aboard</h2>\n<p>Reaching the person is only half the job. First attach them to the boat with a line, so they cannot drift away. Then decide how to lift them.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><b>Freeboard.</b> The height of the deck above the water is often more than a metre on a modern yacht. A tired, cold person in wet clothes cannot climb that on their own.</li>\n<li><b>Lifesling.</b> This floating sling on a long line goes under the person's arms. Once they are alongside, clip a spare halyard (a line used to hoist sails) to the sling and winch them up.</li>\n<li><b>Bathing ladder.</b> In calm water the stern ladder can be the easiest way up, but only with the engine stopped. In waves the stern pitches hard, so take great care.</li>\n<li><b>Lifting with care.</b> Someone who has been in the water for a while should be lifted as horizontally as possible, for example with a second line under the knees, and then kept lying down.</li>\n</ul>\n</section>\n<section id=\"cold\">\n<h2>Cold shock and hypothermia</h2>\n<p>Sudden immersion causes cold shock: an involuntary gasp and fast breathing in the first minute or so. This is when people swallow water, and why a lifejacket that holds the face clear matters. Even in summer, Mediterranean water is cooler than the body, and after a long time in it a person becomes hypothermic (dangerously cold), weak and confused.</p>\n<p>Once aboard, get them out of the wind, remove wet clothes and wrap them in dry layers or a sleeping bag. Give warm, sweet drinks only if they are fully conscious. Warm them gradually, keep them lying down and watch them closely. If they were unconscious, breathed in water or stay very cold, get medical advice by radio.</p>\n</section>\n<section id=\"practise\">\n<h2>Practise it on the water</h2>\n<p>On the Introduction to Yachting course we drop a fender and bucket over the side without warning, and the crew runs the whole drill: shout, throw, point, press MOB. Everyone takes turns as spotter and at the helm, recovering under engine, with a quick-stop and with reach-tack-reach, until each of you can stop the boat beside the fender.</p>\n</section>\n\n          <div class=\"art-mobile-course\"><div class=\"band art-course\"><span class=\"kicker\">Practise it on</span><div class=\"card-title\">Introduction to Yachting</div><p>Learn this on the water with an instructor, in a week living aboard.</p><a class=\"pill pill-primary pill-wide pill-sm\" href=\"/en#courses\">See the courses</a></div></div>\n        </div>\n        <aside class=\"art-side\" aria-label=\"On this page\"><span class=\"kicker\">On this page</span><ol class=\"toc-list\"><li><a href=\"#prevention\">Staying on board</a></li><li><a href=\"#first-seconds\">The first seconds</a></li><li><a href=\"#mayday\">When to call for help</a></li><li><a href=\"#engine\">Recovery under engine</a></li><li><a href=\"#quick-stop\">Quick-stop and heave-to</a></li><li><a href=\"#reach-tack-reach\">Reach, tack, reach</a></li><li><a href=\"#aboard\">Getting them aboard</a></li><li><a href=\"#cold\">Cold shock and hypothermia</a></li><li><a href=\"#practise\">Practise it on the water</a></li></ol><div class=\"band art-course\"><span class=\"kicker\">Practise it on</span><div class=\"card-title\">Introduction to Yachting</div><p>Learn this on the water with an instructor, in a week living aboard.</p><a class=\"pill pill-primary pill-wide pill-sm\" href=\"/en#courses\">See the courses</a></div></aside>\n      </div>\n    </article>\n    <section class=\"sec\" style=\"margin-top:96px\">\n      <div class=\"sec-head row-head\"><div><span class=\"kicker\">Theory</span><h2 class=\"h2\" style=\"margin-top:12px\">Keep reading</h2></div><div class=\"row-nav\"><a class=\"pill pill-quiet\" href=\"/en/theory\">All theory</a></div></div>\n      <div class=\"hscroll\" tabindex=\"0\" aria-label=\"More theory\"><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/vhf-procedure\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1528154291023-a6525fabe5b4?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A boat seen from above on dark water\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">All crew</span><span class=\"card-title\">VHF radio: Mayday, Pan-Pan and everyday calls</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Marine VHF procedure in plain English: channel 16, DSC distress alerts, the phonetic alphabet, Mayday and Pan-Pan formats with examples, and marina calls.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>5 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/safety-on-board\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1542405242-8217b4c9e34c?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A crew in life jackets on deck\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">All crew</span><span class=\"card-title\">Safety on board: the gear and the briefing</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Yacht safety for crew: the skipper's briefing, 150N lifejackets and harnesses, the liferaft and grab bag, EPIRB and PLB, flares, fire, gas and seacocks.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/points-of-sail\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1540946485063-a40da27545f8?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A sailing yacht close-hauled on open water\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Points of sail: how a yacht sails close to the wind</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Learn the points of sail from close-hauled to running: the no-go zone, sail trim on each point, tacking and gybing, apparent wind and reading telltales.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/knots\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1563296291-14f26f10c20f?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"A schooner with all sails set\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Six knots every crew member uses</span><span class=\"card-meta\">Six sailing knots every crew member needs: bowline, clove hitch, round turn and two half hitches, cleat hitch, figure-of-eight and reef knot, step by step.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/tacking-gybing\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777880652269-ff1dd5e65fbb?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"Two sailors at the wheel under sail\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Beginner</span><span class=\"card-title\">Tacking and gybing, step by step</span><span class=\"card-meta\">How to tack and gybe a sailing yacht: the calls, crew jobs, winches without riding turns, a controlled gybe, getting out of irons and tips for catamarans.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a><a class=\"card\" href=\"/en/theory/reefing\"><div class=\"photo\"><div class=\"clip\"><img src=\"https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1777404380697-6b0725b148a7?w=900&q=80&auto=format&fit=crop\" alt=\"Two crew working at the mast in a fresh breeze\" loading=\"lazy\"></div></div><div class=\"card-body\"><span class=\"lvl\">Bareboat skipper</span><span class=\"card-title\">Reefing: when and how to shorten sail</span><span class=\"card-meta\">When and how to reef a sailing yacht: signs it is time, slab reefing step by step, in-mast furling mains, rolling in the genoa, sail balance and shaking out.</span><div class=\"facts\"><span class=\"fact\"><svg class=\"i\"><use href=\"#i-clip\"/></svg>6 min read</span></div></div></a></div>\n    </section>\n  ", "footer": "<footer class=\"site-foot\">\n    <div class=\"foot-row\">\n      <span class=\"meta\">Ocean Way · Layout prototype. Prices, dates and people are sample content. Photos via Unsplash.</span>\n      <nav class=\"foot-links\"><a href=\"#\">About</a><a href=\"#\">Telegram</a><a href=\"#\">Privacy</a></nav>\n    </div>\n  </footer>", "scripts": ["\nconst root = document.documentElement;\ndocument.getElementById('themeBtn').addEventListener('click', () => { if (root.dataset.theme === 'night') delete root.dataset.theme; else root.dataset.theme = 'night'; });\nif (new URLSearchParams(location.search).get('theme') === 'night') root.dataset.theme = 'night';\n(() => {\n  const links = [...document.querySelectorAll('.art-side .toc-list a')]; if (!links.length) return;\n  const secs = links.map((a) => document.getElementById(a.getAttribute('href').slice(1)));\n  const io = new IntersectionObserver((es) => es.forEach((e) => { if (e.isIntersecting) links.forEach((a, i) => a.classList.toggle('on', secs[i] === e.target)); }), { root